## 6. SELF-LEARNING ENGINE

Build one shared learning infrastructure for all 30 indicators.

Do not create 30 unrelated fake AI learning systems.

### Learning pipeline

1. Collect verified historical and live market data.
2. Calculate indicator features.
3. Generate timestamped signals.
4. Record the exact conditions at signal time.
5. Track subsequent price movement.
6. Determine whether targets or stop-losses were reached.
7. Calculate outcome statistics.
8. Group outcomes by instrument, timeframe, session and market regime.
9. Train or update supported models.
10. Validate against unseen historical periods.
11. Compare candidate models with production versions.
12. Publish approved versions.
13. Monitor live performance.
14. Roll back if performance deteriorates.

### Learning capabilities

- Historical pattern learning.
- Adaptive parameter optimisation.
- Market-regime learning.
- Instrument-specific learning.
- Timeframe-specific learning.
- Session-specific learning.
- False-signal recognition.
- Signal quality scoring.
- Entry sensitivity optimisation.
- Stop-loss and target research.
- Performance decay detection.
- Model comparison.
- Walk-forward testing.
- Out-of-sample validation.
- Version control.

### Learning dashboard

Display:
- Active learning jobs.
- Completed learning jobs.
- Model version.
- Last training date.
- Training sample size.
- Validation sample size.
- Previous performance.
- Current performance.
- Proposed parameter changes.
- Model confidence.
- Learning errors.
- Deployment approval status.

### Learning protection

Never:
- Train using future information unavailable at signal time.
- Use future candles to confirm historical entries.
- Repaint previously confirmed signals.
- Optimise solely for win rate.
- Automatically increase trading risk after wins.
- Change production parameters after every trade.
- Present insufficient sample sizes as reliable results.

Use realistic spread, commission, slippage and execution assumptions.

